BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze how 'Customer Journey Mapping' identifies high-leverage friction points in omnichannel digital healthcare patient onboarding.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. The Omnichannel Healthcare Journey Topology:) Tj
T*
(   - Touchpoints span physical and digital realms: Symptoms -> Web Search -> Provider Directory ->) Tj
T*
(   Online Booking -> SMS Appointment Reminders -> Hospital Check-In Kiosk -> Waiting Room ->) Tj
T*
(   Doctor Consultation -> Pharmacy Pickup -> Post-Visit Billing Portal.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Mapping the Multi-Layer Journey Architecture:) Tj
T*
(   - Layer 1: Journey Stages \(Awareness, Booking, Check-In, Clinical Care, Post-Care\).) Tj
T*
(   - Layer 2: Patient Actions & Touchpoints at each stage.) Tj
T*
(   - Layer 3: Patient Emotional Journey \(Emotional Highs and Lows curve\).) Tj
T*
(   - Layer 4: Pain Points & Hidden Anxieties.) Tj
T*
(   - Layer 5: Backstage Supporting Systems \(EHR APIs, SMS gateways, insurance eligibility) Tj
T*
(   clearinghouses\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Identifying the 'Valley of Despair' Friction Point:) Tj
T*
(   - The Discovery: The emotional curve plunges into acute negative territory between Online) Tj
T*
(   Booking and Hospital Check-In.) Tj
T*
(   - The Root Cause: The patient spent 20 minutes filling out medical history on the digital web) Tj
T*
(   portal at home. Upon arriving at the clinic, the receptionist hands them a physical clipboard) Tj
T*
(   with 5 paper forms, demanding that they rewrite their entire medical history by hand!) Tj
T*
(   - Systemic Redesign: Connecting the web portal directly to the hospital EHR database, replacing) Tj
T*
(   clipboards with a 10-second biometric check-in, eliminating redundant data entry and) Tj
T*
(   transforming patient trust.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 17:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Evaluate the 'Three Mile Island' nuclear control room disaster through the lens of Human Factors Engineering and Nielsen's Usability Heuristics.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Incident Overview \(March 28, 1979\):) Tj
T*
(   - Unit 2 reactor at Three Mile Island experienced a cooling system malfunction. A) Tj
T*
(   pilot-operated relief valve \(PORV\) opened to relieve pressure, but became physically stuck) Tj
T*
(   open, allowing radioactive cooling water to boil away, causing partial reactor core meltdown.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Catastrophic Breakdown of Usability Heuristics:) Tj
T*
(   - Heuristic 1 \(Visibility of System Status - The Fatal Flaw\):) Tj
T*
(   * The control panel had a light labeled 'PORV'. When the light went out, operators assumed the) Tj
T*
(   valve was closed.) Tj
T*
(   * The Engineering Deception: The light did not monitor physical valve position; it merely) Tj
T*
(   monitored whether electrical power was applied to the solenoid! The valve was physically stuck) Tj
T*
ET

