(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Foundations of Cognitive Walkthrough:) Tj
T*
(   - A task-centered, expert review method focusing specifically on novice user discoverability) Tj
T*
(   and learnability through exploration.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. The Four Foundational Questions Applied to GitHub SSH Key Setup:) Tj
T*
(   - Step: The user pastes their `id_rsa.pub` key into the 'Key' textarea and clicks 'Add SSH) Tj
T*
(   Key'.) Tj
T*
(   - Question 1: Will the user try to achieve the right effect?) Tj
T*
(   * Analysis: Novice developers understand they want to push code, but rarely understand) Tj
T*
(   cryptographic public/private key pairs. Many mistakenly attempt to paste their *private* key) Tj
T*
(   \(`id_rsa`\).) Tj
T*
(   - Question 2: Will the user notice that the correct action is available?) Tj
T*
(   * Analysis: The 'New SSH Key' button is located under 'Settings > SSH and GPG Keys'. A novice) Tj
T*
(   hunting in the repository settings rather than account-level settings will fail to notice it.) Tj
T*
(   - Question 3: Will the user associate the correct action with the effect they desire?) Tj
T*
(   * Analysis: The button is labeled 'New SSH Key'. Novices looking for 'Password setup' or) Tj
T*
(   'Terminal authorization' may hesitate due to terminology mismatch.) Tj
T*
(   - Question 4: If the correct action is performed, will the user see that progress is being) Tj
T*
(   made?) Tj
T*
(   * Analysis: Once added, GitHub displays the key's SHA256 fingerprint with a green key icon,) Tj
T*
(   confirming success. However, until the user runs `ssh -T git@github.com`, they receive zero) Tj
T*
(   feedback confirming that their local terminal can authenticate.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Engineering Redesign:) Tj
T*
(   - Add explicit inline warnings: 'Ensure you paste your PUBLIC key \(ending in .pub\), never your) Tj
T*
(   private key', and provide a 1-click terminal verification command.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
