(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the 'Formative vs. Summative' usability testing dichotomy. In which phases of the agile product development lifecycle should each be deployed, and what metrics govern each?) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Conceptual and Operational Dichotomy:) Tj
T*
(   - Formative Usability Testing \(Diagnostic / Generative\):) Tj
T*
(   * Timing: Iterative, continuous deployment during early and middle design phases \(Sprints 1) Tj
T*
(   through 10\).) Tj
T*
(   * Focus: Qualitative discovery: 'Why is the user stumbling? Where is the mental model mismatch?) Tj
T*
(   What needs to be redesigned?') Tj
T*
(   * Artifacts Tested: Low-to-medium fidelity wireframes, clickable prototypes, paper mockups.) Tj
T*
(   * Metrics: Qualitative feedback, verbal Think-Aloud quotes, Feedback Capture Matrix clusters.) Tj
T*
(   - Summative Usability Testing \(Evaluative / Benchmarking\):) Tj
T*
(   * Timing: Deployed at the end of a major release cycle, before launch \(Release Candidate) Tj
T*
(   milestone\).) Tj
T*
(   * Focus: Quantitative validation: 'Does the software satisfy our contractual usability) Tj
T*
(   benchmarks and regulatory SLAs?') Tj
T*
(   * Artifacts Tested: Fully integrated, production-grade staging software running on real) Tj
T*
(   devices.) Tj
T*
(   * Metrics: Statistical metrics: Task Completion Rate \(%\), Time-on-Task \(seconds\), Error) Tj
T*
(   Frequency, System Usability Scale \(SUS\) score, Single Ease Question \(SEQ\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Agile Lifecycle Synchronization:) Tj
T*
(   - Formative testing informs feature shaping during active development; Summative testing serves) Tj
T*
(   as the non-negotiable quality gate that grants or blocks production release clearance.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 24:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Detail the 'Heuristic Evaluation' methodology developed by Jakob Nielsen and Rolf Molich. Outline the four-stage evaluation process and explain why multiple independent evaluators are required.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Architecture of Heuristic Evaluation:) Tj
T*
(   - An expert inspection method where usability professionals systematically evaluate an) Tj
T*
(   interface against established, battle-tested usability principles \(Nielsen's 10 Heuristics\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. The Four-Stage Evaluation Process:) Tj
T*
(   - Stage 1: Pre-Evaluation Training & Briefing: Evaluators are aligned on the target domain,) Tj
T*
(   user personas, operational context, and core scenario paths.) Tj
T*
(   - Stage 2: Independent Expert Inspection:) Tj
T*
(   * Crucial Requirement: Each evaluator inspects the interface in complete isolation, performing) Tj
T*
(   tasks and tagging every usability violation with the specific heuristic number it violates,) Tj
T*
(   assigning a severity rating \(0 = Cosmetic to 4 = Usability Catastrophe\).) Tj
T*
(   * Evaluators must NEVER communicate or collaborate during this phase to preserve independent) Tj
T*
(   observation.) Tj
T*
(   - Stage 3: Aggregation & Synthesis: The team gathers to pool all unique findings into a single) Tj
T*
(   master defect log, consolidating duplicates and calculating average severity scores.) Tj
T*
(   - Stage 4: Debriefing & Recommendations: Evaluators meet with software development teams to) Tj
T*
(   brainstorm concrete engineering countermeasures for high-severity defects.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Why Multiple Evaluators are Mandatory \(The Poisson Model\):) Tj
T*
(   - A single evaluator catches only approximately 35% of usability defects, as individual experts) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
