(   [Problem Statement]: Detail the 'SUS Score Adjective Rating Scale' developed by Bangor, Kortum, and Miller. How does an engineering team translate raw SUS numerical scores into executive business decisions?) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Bangor, Kortum, and Miller's Empirical Calibration \(2008\):) Tj
T*
(   - Analyzing over 2,300 usability tests, researchers established a standardized empirical) Tj
T*
(   mapping between raw SUS numerical scores \(0-100\), academic letter grades \(A through F\), and) Tj
T*
(   human adjective ratings:) Tj
T*
(   * SUS 85.0 to 100.0: Grade A+ / Excellent \(Best-in-class product\)) Tj
T*
(   * SUS 80.3 to 84.9: Grade A / Good) Tj
T*
(   * SUS 68.0 to 80.2: Grade B/C / Okay \(68.0 is the empirical average baseline\)) Tj
T*
(   * SUS 51.0 to 67.9: Grade D / Poor \(Marginal usability; high customer friction\)) Tj
T*
(   * SUS 0.0 to 50.9: Grade F / Awful \(Unacceptable; catastrophic churn risk\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Translating SUS into Executive Business Decisions:) Tj
T*
(   - Release Gate Enforcement: Establishing non-negotiable launch criteria: 'No major enterprise) Tj
T*
(   software release may ship to general availability if its summative SUS score is below 72.') Tj
T*
(   - Allocating Technical vs. Design Sprints: If SUS drops into the Marginal category \(55-65\),) Tj
T*
(   engineering leadership freezes new feature development and dedicates two full sprints) Tj
T*
(   exclusively to UX refactoring and error recovery.) Tj
T*
(   - Correlating with Commercial Churn: Proving to CFOs that products scoring SUS > 80 experience) Tj
T*
(   40% lower customer churn, turning usability investment into a quantifiable revenue preservation) Tj
T*
(   strategy.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 28:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze how 'Cognitive Load Theory' \(John Sweller\) guides the design of developer error diagnostics in modern CLI compilers \(e.g., Elm and Rust\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Sweller's Cognitive Load Triad in Compiler Diagnostics:) Tj
T*
(   - Intrinsic Cognitive Load: The inherent difficulty of understanding the logic bug in the) Tj
T*
(   user's code.) Tj
T*
(   - Extraneous Cognitive Load: Mental effort wasted deciphering cryptic, hostile compiler error) Tj
T*
(   codes, unformatted stack traces, and ambiguous line numbers.) Tj
T*
(   - Germane Cognitive Load: Mental energy devoted to schema formation and learning the language's) Tj
T*
(   memory safety rules.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Elm and Rust's Compiler Redesign:) Tj
T*
(   - Eliminating Extraneous Load:) Tj
T*
(   * Compilers historically dumped 50 lines of C++ template syntax errors. Elm and Rust completely) Tj
T*
(   eliminated cryptic syntax dumps, replacing them with conversational human language.) Tj
T*
(   * ASCII Art Visual Annotations: Highlighting the exact line of code with visual carets \(`^^^`\)) Tj
T*
(   and distinct colors, showing the exact character where the type mismatch occurred.) Tj
T*
(   - Stimulating Germane Schema Construction:) Tj
T*
(   * Proactive Suggestions \(`help:`\): The compiler does not merely state that the code failed; it) Tj
T*
(   suggests the exact code fix: 'did you mean to borrow this reference? Try adding `&`'.) Tj
T*
(   * Interactive Educational Flags: Running `rustc --explain E0382` opens a full interactive) Tj
T*
(   tutorial explaining the underlying ownership and borrow mechanics.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

endstream
